	(text "Front-panel vector board.\n\nOff-PCB. Connects to the main board's RJ45 J4 (on the analog board's\ninterface sheet) through a shielded CAT6 umbilical (T568B). See\nDocumentation/front_panel_interface.md for wiring, grounding rules,\nand the open-items checklist.\n\nI2C bus (shared with the analog board via CAT6 pair 2):\n  0x20  PCF8575 -- this board (LCD + LEDs + optional paddle)\n  0x21  PCF8575 -- analog board, relay drives          [not on this sheet]\n  0x36  MAX17048 -- Metro on-board fuel gauge          [not on this sheet]\n  0x37  I2C QT rotary -- STEP  (A0 jumper closed to move off 0x36 / MAX17048)\n  0x38  I2C QT rotary -- FUNC  (A1 jumper closed)\n  0x60  Si5351A VFO                                    [not on this sheet]\n  0x67  MCP4728 quad DAC (post-EEPROM reprogram from factory 0x60)\n                                                       [not on this sheet]\n\nParts to add here (vector board, behind LCD):\n  - Adafruit PCF8575 I2C 16-GPIO expander (PID 5904) at 0x20 -- drives\n    the LCD in 4-bit mode, reads the paddle (option 2 below), and\n    drives the front-panel status LEDs.\n  - 2x Adafruit I2C QT Rotary Encoder breakouts (PID 4991) at 0x37\n    (STEP) and 0x38 (FUNC), each carrying a Bourns PEC11-4 encoder\n    with pushbutton. Onboard seesaw SAMD09 handles quadrature\n    decoding + button debounce.\n  - Winstar WH2004A 20x4 character LCD (4-bit mode via the PCF8575).\n  - MBL-600 optical encoder (RS-422 differential -- wires straight\n    through the CAT6 to the main-board receiver on the interface\n    sheet; does NOT pass through the PCF8575).\n  - Front-panel LEDs (TX, fault, VFO-lock) on spare PCF8575 GPIO.\n  - Bypass caps at LCD Vcc (10 uF + 100 nF). The PCF8575 and QT\n    encoder breakouts have their own bypassing + I2C pull-ups\n    on-board.\n  - LCD contrast trim pot, backlight series resistor.\n\nPaddle jack -- two possible paths, mechanical build decides:\n\n  1. Analog board (as currently wired for Rev A). Jack lives at\n     connector J10 on the analog board's arduino sheet; DIT / DAH\n     go directly to Metro D5 / D6 (labels PADDLE_A / PADDLE_B).\n     No I2C latency; interrupt-capable in firmware.\n\n  2. Front panel (this sheet, reserved as an option). Jack wires\n     DIT / DAH into two spare PCF8575 GPIOs; firmware polls the\n     expander (already polled for LCD / LEDs). ~10 ms worst-case\n     latency at 100 Hz poll. No spare CAT6 conductors are needed --\n     signal travels back over the existing I2C pair.\n\n  Only ONE path should be populated in any given build to avoid\n  double-key events in firmware."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
